<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Electrolytes at a glance">
  <div><strong>They are ions</strong><span>Electrolytes are substances that form charged particles in solution, not a special category of sports-drink ingredient.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Location matters</strong><span>Sodium and chloride dominate extracellular fluid, while potassium is concentrated mainly inside cells.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Balance matters</strong><span>Both unusually low and unusually high electrolyte concentrations can impair normal physiology.</span></div>
</div>

<div class="bhd-page__prose-grid">
  <article class="bhd-page__prose">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">The core idea</p>
    <h2>Body water is never just water</h2>
    <p>Blood plasma, the fluid between cells and the fluid inside cells all contain dissolved ions, proteins, nutrients and metabolic products. The concentration gradients between these spaces are actively maintained and are essential to normal cell function.</p>
    <p>The extracellular compartment is relatively rich in <strong>sodium, chloride and bicarbonate</strong>. The intracellular compartment is relatively rich in <strong>potassium</strong>, along with phosphate, magnesium and negatively charged proteins. Cell membranes and transport proteins maintain these differences rather than allowing every ion to equalize everywhere.</p>
  </article>
  <aside class="bhd-page__callout">
    <span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Useful distinction</span>
    <h3>Electrolyte balance is not the same as “more electrolytes.”</h3>
    <p>The body tightly regulates electrolyte concentrations. The goal of a hydration strategy is not to maximize ions, but to replace what is appropriate for the situation without creating excessive intake.</p>
  </aside>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Major ions in hydration</p>
  <h2>Four electrolytes central to the Best Hydrate formula</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Ion</th><th>Where it is concentrated</th><th>Broad physiological role</th><th>Best Hydrate ingredient source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Sodium (Na<sup>+</sup>)</strong></td><td>Main extracellular cation</td><td>Helps determine extracellular fluid osmolality and volume; contributes to electrical signalling and coupled transport.</td><td>Sodium chloride and sodium bicarbonate</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Potassium (K<sup>+</sup>)</strong></td><td>Main intracellular cation</td><td>Important to membrane potential, nerve conduction and muscle-cell function.</td><td>Potassium chloride</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Chloride (Cl<sup>−</sup>)</strong></td><td>Major extracellular anion</td><td>Contributes to electroneutrality, osmotic balance and several transport processes.</td><td>Sodium chloride and potassium chloride</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Bicarbonate (HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup>)</strong></td><td>Important extracellular buffer</td><td>Part of the carbon dioxide/bicarbonate buffering system that helps regulate acid-base balance.</td><td>Sodium bicarbonate</td></tr>
