  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Citrate chemistry</p>
    <h2>Where citric acid fits</h2>
    <p>The current Best Hydrate product description lists citric acid among its electrolyte ingredients. In water, citric acid participates in acid-base equilibria and contributes citrate-containing ionic species depending on the solution’s pH.</p>
    <p>Citric acid also has a practical formulation role in flavour and acidity. That is different from claiming that citrate replaces the physiological roles of sodium, potassium, chloride or bicarbonate.</p>
    <a class="bhd-page__text-link" href="/pages/ingredient-rationale">See the ingredient rationale <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__feature">
  <div>
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Gradients drive function</p>
    <h2>Sodium outside, potassium inside</h2>
    <p>One of the most important features of electrolyte physiology is that sodium and potassium are distributed very differently across cell membranes. The sodium-potassium ATPase uses cellular energy to move sodium out of cells and potassium into cells, helping maintain the gradients used for membrane potential, transport and cell volume regulation.</p>
    <p>This is why an electrolyte list is only the beginning of the story. <strong>Concentration, compartment and transport</strong> determine what those ions actually do.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">When replacement matters more</p>
  <h2>Losses are not identical from one situation to another</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4">
    <article><h3>Sweat</h3><p>Sweat contains water and electrolytes, especially sodium and chloride. Both sweat rate and sweat sodium concentration vary widely.</p><a href="/pages/heat-high-sweat">Heat &amp; high sweat</a></article>
    <article><h3>GI losses</h3><p>Vomiting and diarrhoea can cause clinically important losses of water and electrolytes and may require therapeutic rehydration.</p><a href="/pages/clinical-hydration">Clinical hydration</a></article>
    <article><h3>Food intake</h3><p>Ordinary meals supply substantial electrolytes. A beverage should be considered alongside the rest of the diet, not in isolation.</p><a href="/pages/everyday-wellness">Everyday wellness</a></article>
    <article><h3>Kidney regulation</h3><p>Healthy kidneys continually adjust sodium, potassium and water excretion, while disease and some medications can change this balance.</p><a href="/pages/how-hydration-works">How hydration works</a></article>
  </div>
</section>

